-## Subtopics
-
-### InnerGS Framework
-The broader framework for internal scenes reconstruction and segmentation via factorized 3D Gaussian Splatting. It provides the foundation for integrating VLMs. [InnerGS](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2508.13287)
-
-### Knowledge-Enhanced Medical VLM
-The source of high-dimensional embeddings. Specifically referenced as Zhao et al., 2025 [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Scene-Specific Autoencoder
-The mechanism for distilling high-dimensional embeddings into compact latent attributes. Specifically referenced as Qin et al., 2024 [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Empty Gaussians**: If $N$ is empty, $\mathbf{F}(v) = \mathbf{0}$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: The method relies on the accuracy of the autoencoder's reconstruction. Distortion in the latent space may affect segmentation quality [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Efficiency:** Significantly reduces memory usage compared to explicit high-dimensional modeling by using compact latent attributes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance (CT-ORG subset of SAT-DS):**
-    *   Mean Dice Similarity Coefficient (mDSC): 88.17% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Mean Intersection-over-Union (mIoU): 83.72% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Pixel Accuracy (PA): 98.97% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Liver DSC: Consistently above 84.62% [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Top 5 Slices Performance (CT-ORG):**
-    *   Slice 32: PA 99.42%, mIoU 95.31%, mDSC 99.42%, Liver DSC 98.18% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Slice 70: PA 98.81%, mIoU 94.32%, mDSC 98.81%, Liver DSC 97.13% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Slice 29: PA 99.20%, mIoU 99.20%, mDSC 99.20%, Liver DSC 96.26% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Slice 65: PA 98.62%, mIoU 98.62%, mDSC 98.62%, Liver DSC 97.40% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Slice 30: PA 99.16%, mIoU 99.16%, mDSC 99.16%, Liver DSC 94.67% [S1, unofficial]
-    *   Note: Some slices in the source table list "Others DSC" instead of "Liver DSC" for certain rows, but the primary metrics are consistent [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables precise 3D segmentation of Regions of Interest through open-vocabulary queries; preserves volumetric consistency; computationally efficient [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained knowledge-enhanced medical VLM and a scene-specific autoencoder; performance is evaluated on medical CT data (CT-ORG), so generalization to other domains is not explicitly stated [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory**: Significantly reduces memory usage compared to storing high-dimensional VLM embeddings for each Gaussian, as it uses compact latent attributes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: On the CT-ORG subset of SAT-DS, the method achieves a mean Dice Similarity Coefficient (mDSC) of 88.17%, mean Intersection-over-Union (mIoU) of 83.72%, and Pixel Accuracy (PA) of 98.97% [S1, unofficial]. Liver DSC is consistently above 84.62% [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Enables open-vocabulary queries without retraining the 3D model; preserves volumetric consistency; computationally efficient due to compact latent space [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Performance depends on the quality of the distilled latent features and the decoder; specific to the domain of the VLM (e.g., medical) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is part of the InnerGS framework, introduced in "InnerGS: Internal Scenes Reconstruction and Segmentation via Factorized 3D Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. It extends 3D Gaussian Splatting to facilitate multimodal medical scene understanding by integrating Vision-Language Models (VLMs) [S1, unofficial]. The approach builds upon the potential for downstream clinical tasks, specifically targeting open-vocabulary 3D segmentation [S1, unofficial]. The VLM reference is Zhao et al., 2025, and the autoencoder reference is Qin et al., 2024 [S1, unofficial]. The dataset used for evaluation is the CT-ORG subset in SAT-DS, licensed under CC BY 3.0 [S1, unofficial].
+The method is part of the InnerGS framework, which extends 3D Gaussian Splatting for medical scene understanding [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the potential for downstream clinical tasks by integrating Vision-Language Models (VLMs) [S1, unofficial]. The approach was proposed to circumvent the memory explosion of explicit high-dimensional modeling while preserving volumetric consistency [S1, unofficial]. It references Zhao et al. (2025) for the knowledge-enhanced medical VLM and Qin et al. (2024) for the scene-specific autoencoder [S1, unofficial]. The dataset used for evaluation is CT-ORG, licensed under CC BY 3.0 [S1, unofficial].
 
